<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Sever</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Sever is a town in the Landes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adour">Adour</a> river. It grew around a Benedictine abbey founded in 988 by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-sanchez-of-gascony">William Sánchez of Gascony</a>, whose scriptorium produced the illuminated Saint-Sever Beatus in the 11th century, a major work of Romanesque art. The abbey church is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listing of the routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-sever.jpg" alt="Saint-Sever" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jibi44 · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbaye_St_Sever_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
